menu "espOS core"

    config ESPOS_CORE_HEALTH_WATCHDOG
        bool "Restart on health conditions the device cannot recover from"
        default y
        help
            When on, espos_init() arms espos_health's watchdog policy: the
            device restarts after three consecutive 10 s ticks with a
            condition in ALARM that was reported with
            ESPOS_HEALTH_F_REBOOT_ON_ALARM (internal RAM exhausted, the
            SignalK stream dead for sk.stall_s while the network is up, a
            watched task stalled). A network loss never counts. The per-app
            espos_start_opts_t.health_watchdog can still turn it off at
            runtime. Details: docs/health.md.

            Turn it off for a device whose application handles such
            conditions itself, or where a restart would be worse than the
            condition (a display mid-passage).

    config ESPOS_WIFI
        bool "Start the WiFi station manager (espos_wifi) in espos_start()"
        default y
        depends on SOC_WIFI_SUPPORTED || SOC_WIRELESS_HOST_SUPPORTED
        help
            espos_start() brings espos_wifi up right after the network seam
            (espos_net) on every chip that has a radio, or — the ESP32-P4 —
            a co-processor it drives through esp_wifi_remote. Turn this off
            for a firmware that links espos_wifi but carries its network on
            another transport (an Ethernet gateway): the station, its
            portal and the /wifi endpoints then never start, and nothing
            else changes — SignalK, OTA and mDNS follow espos_net, not the
            radio. Not offered on chips without WiFi (the 802.15.4-only
            H-series), where espos_wifi is not built at all.

    config ESPOS_ETH
        bool "Start wired Ethernet (espos_eth) in espos_start()"
        default y
        depends on ETH_USE_ESP32_EMAC
        help
            espos_start() brings espos_eth up right after the network seam,
            beside the WiFi station rather than instead of it: espos_net
            routes over the cable whenever it has a link, and over the
            station otherwise. Only matters in a firmware that builds
            espos_eth, on a chip with an internal EMAC.

            A failure here does not stop espos_start(). A PHY that does not
            answer -- a board without one, a wrong reset GPIO -- is logged
            and the device carries on, because a firmware that could have
            come up on WiFi should not reboot-loop over a missing cable
            interface.

            Turn this off for an application that starts Ethernet itself,
            for instance only after enabling a board power rail.

endmenu
